# EP02 — How to Survive the Wendigo (According to Folklore)
# id | picture | tokens | caption | narration      (ward lines: id | picture | ward ... | NUMERAL | rule | narration)
# Respect note: the windigo (wendigo, witiko) is a living teaching of Algonquian-speaking peoples (Anishinaabe/Ojibwe,
# Cree, Innu and others), not a public-domain movie monster. The script leans on Anishinaabe writer Basil Johnston and on
# ethnohistorians, retells no sacred ceremony, and keeps the people in it as people, not props.
# [S1] Basil Johnston, "The Manitous: The Spiritual World of the Ojibway" (HarperCollins, 1995), chapter "Weendigo": a giant, gaunt, ash-grey figure with torn lips; it grows as it eats, so it is never full; a teaching against excess and greed.
# [S2] Wikipedia, "Wendigo": Algonquian origin; associations with winter, famine, cannibalism and greed; humans can become wendigo, especially after eating human flesh; a heart of ice in many accounts; antlered portrayals are modern pop culture. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wendigo
# [S3] Robert A. Brightman, "The Windigo in the Material World", Ethnohistory 35:4 (1988), 337-379: windigo accounts, famine conditions in the subarctic fur-trade era, and people who feared they were turning. https://doi.org/10.2307/482140
# [S4] Lou Marano, "Windigo Psychosis: The Anatomy of an Emic-Etic Confusion", Current Anthropology 23:4 (1982), 385-412: argues that most recorded "windigo" cases were killings of people accused of becoming windigo, not cannibals. https://doi.org/10.1086/202868
# [S5] Swift Runner (Ka-Ki-Si-Kutchin), Cree man convicted of killing and eating members of his family; hanged at Fort Saskatchewan on 20 December 1879; food was available nearby. Wikipedia, "Swift Runner" https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swift_Runner ; Mysteries of Canada, "Swift Runner" https://mysteriesofcanada.com/alberta/wendigo-encounters-in-canada-episode-3-swift-runner
# [S6] Dictionary of Canadian Biography, "Zhauwuno-geezhigo-gaubow" (Jack Fiddler): Oji-Cree leader at Sandy Lake, said to have defeated many windigos; arrested by the North-West Mounted Police in June 1907; died by suicide in custody that September; his brother Joseph (Pesequan) died in custody in 1909. http://www.biographi.ca/en/bio/zhauwuno_geezhigo_gaubow_13E.html ; Wikipedia, "Jack Fiddler" https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jack_Fiddler
# [S7] Nathan D. Carlson, "Reviving Witiko (Windigo): An Ethnohistory of 'Cannibal Monsters' in the Athabasca District of Northern Alberta, 1878-1910", Ethnohistory 56:3 (2009), 355-394. https://doi.org/10.1215/00141801-2009-001

# ---- HOOK ----
w01 | a vast frozen boreal forest of black spruce trees under heavy snow, a tiny lone cabin, wide scene | snow night zoomin | February | It's February, somewhere in the northern forest. The snow is deeper than you are tall. And the food ran out eleven days ago.
w02 | a lone traveller in a heavy fur hood and snowshoes, hunched against the wind, full figure | walk snow night | | You've boiled your moccasin leather. You've scraped lichen off the rocks. And tonight, for the first time, sitting by the fire, you look at the person next to you, and you notice a thought that frightens you.
w03 | a gaunt towering emaciated giant with ash grey skin pulled tight over its bones and torn lips, in a snowy forest, frightening, full figure | loom snow night | | The people who have lived in these forests for thousands of years have a name for what that thought can turn into.
w04 | =w03 | zoomin night | | Windigo. Wendigo. Witiko. A giant of hunger, made of frost and bone, that eats people, and is never, ever full.
w05 | =w01 | panr snow night | | And here's what makes it different from almost every other monster in the Ward Book. You don't only meet the Wendigo out in the woods. Sometimes, you become it.
w06 | an open old leather-bound book with blank pages and a melting candle beside it, snowflakes | title snow | the Wendigo | This is the Ward Book. And tonight, how to survive the Wendigo.

# ---- WHOSE LEGEND THIS IS ----
w07 | a map-like woodcut of northern lakes, rivers and pine forests, wide scene | panl dust | The northern forests | First, whose story is this? The Wendigo belongs to Algonquian-speaking peoples of the northern forests. The Anishinaabe, or Ojibwe. The Cree. The Innu. And their neighbours around the Great Lakes and across the subarctic.
w08 | a birch bark canoe resting on the shore of a frozen lake, wide scene | breathe snow | | For them, this isn't a movie monster. It's a teaching, passed down for generations, and it's still told today. So tonight we're leaning on Indigenous writers and careful historians, and we're going to treat it that way.
w09 | an elderly storyteller wrapped in a blanket beside a small fire, full figure, dignified | breathe embers | Basil Johnston | The Anishinaabe writer Basil Johnston described the Weendigo as a giant. Gaunt to the point of starvation. Skin stretched over its bones, the grey colour of ash. Lips torn and bloody.
w10 | =w03 | pulse night | | And here's the detail that matters most. Every time the Weendigo eats, it grows. So its hunger grows with it. It can never catch up. It's starving forever, no matter how much it eats.
w11 | =w09 | zoomin embers | | Johnston saw the lesson clearly. The Weendigo is what excess looks like. Greed, with a body. A hunger that destroys everything it touches, including itself.

# ---- WHERE THE FEAR CAME FROM ----
w12 | an empty cooking pot hanging over a dead fire with snow falling, close up | snow night | Winter famine | To understand the Wendigo, you have to understand winter in the subarctic, before roads, before planes, before grocery stores.
w13 | =w01 | zoomout snow night | | In a bad winter, the animals vanish. Snow traps families in camps far from anyone else. Historians who study the fur trade era have found record after record of hunger so severe that people starved to death in their own lodges.
w14 | =w02 | shiver snow | | And in that kind of hunger, there is one taboo that sits above every other. You do not eat another human being. Not ever. The Wendigo is that taboo, given a face.

# ---- WARD I ----
w15 | two pairs of hands breaking a piece of bannock bread in half to share, close up | ward | I | Share your food | Ward one. Share your food.
w16 | =w15 | zoomin | | This is the deepest rule of all, and it's not about the monster. It's about you. The Wendigo is a spirit of greed. Of keeping more than you need while others go without.
w17 | a small camp of people sitting together around one shared fire in the snow, wide scene | embers snow | | In a world where survival depended on everyone, hoarding food was the first step toward becoming something inhuman. So the first ward is the simplest one. When you have food, share it.

# ---- WARD II ----
w18 | a single cracked human skull lying in the snow beside a bare tree | ward night snow | II | Never cross the line | Ward two. Never cross the line. No matter what.
w19 | =w03 | zoomin night | | In many tellings, the surest way to become a Wendigo is to eat human flesh. Even once. Even starving. Even if the person was already dead.
w20 | =w12 | snow night | | It's a brutal rule, born in brutal winters. But it gave people something to hold on to when everything else had gone. Death, the stories said, is better than becoming the Wendigo.

# ---- WARD III ----
w21 | a man looking into a still pool of water and seeing a monstrous gaunt reflection, full figure | ward fog | III | Watch yourself, not just the woods | Ward three. Watch yourself, not just the woods.
w22 | =w21 | zoomin | | The scariest thing about the Wendigo is that the first signs are inside your own head. In some accounts, the person turning feels sick at the sight of normal food. They grow cold. Distant. And they start to look at people the way a hunter looks at game.
w23 | =w17 | breathe embers | | So communities watched for these signs. And historians have recorded something heartbreaking. People who believed they were turning would sometimes ask their own families to kill them, before they could hurt anyone.
w24 | =w21 | night | | Think about that. The ward isn't a charm you hang on your door. It's paying attention to yourself, and caring enough about the people around you to stop.

# ---- WARD IV ----
w25 | a cooking pot of boiling fat over a roaring fire, steam rising, close up | ward embers | IV | Melt the heart of ice | Ward four. Melt the heart of ice.
w26 | a human heart made of jagged ice crystals, glowing, close up | pulse snow | A heart of ice | In many accounts, a Wendigo has a heart of ice. That's the frozen centre of the monster, and the key to beating it.
w27 | =w25 | zoomin embers | | The stories say it can only truly be destroyed by fire, or by melting that frozen heart. Some tellings speak of hot fat, poured down the throat of someone who was turning, to melt the ice inside before it could take over.
w28 | =w26 | pulse | | Whatever you make of the cure, the symbol is clear. Hunger and greed freeze the heart. Warmth, literal and human, is what thaws it.

# ---- WARD V ----
w29 | a respected elder holding a drum, wearing a fur cap, standing in the snow, dignified, full figure | ward breathe snow | V | Trust the ones who fight it | Ward five. Trust the ones who know how to fight it.
w30 | =w29 | zoomin snow | Jack Fiddler | In the early nineteen hundreds, one of the most famous was Jack Fiddler, an Oji-Cree leader at Sandy Lake, in what's now northern Ontario. He was said to have defeated many Wendigos in his lifetime.
w31 | a red-coated mounted police officer in a wide hat standing stiffly, full figure | breathe | June 1907 | And that's where this legend runs straight into Canadian law. In June nineteen oh seven, the North-West Mounted Police arrested Jack Fiddler and his brother Joseph, for killing a woman their community believed was turning into a Wendigo.
w32 | =w29 | night | | Jack Fiddler took his own life in custody that September. Joseph was convicted, and died in custody in nineteen oh nine. To the police, it was murder. To their community, it was protection. Two worlds, two laws, and one terrible winter story caught between them.

# ---- THE REAL CASES ----
w33 | an old wooden frontier fort with a log palisade in deep snow, wide scene | snow panr | Fort Saskatchewan, 1879 | There's another real case people always bring up. In eighteen seventy-nine, a Cree man known as Swift Runner was hanged at Fort Saskatchewan, in today's Alberta.
w34 | =w33 | zoomin snow night | | He'd killed and eaten members of his own family that winter, and he said a Wendigo spirit had taken hold of him. But the police noted something important. There was food nearby. This wasn't simple starvation.
w35 | an old doctor with spectacles reading a thick book by an oil lamp, full figure | breathe dust | "Wendigo psychosis" | Cases like this led twentieth-century psychiatrists to invent a diagnosis. Wendigo psychosis. A supposed madness, unique to northern peoples, where a person craves human flesh.
w36 | =w35 | zoomin | Lou Marano, 1982 | But in nineteen eighty-two, the anthropologist Lou Marano looked at the records closely, and argued that most so-called Wendigo cases were something else entirely. Not cannibals. People accused of turning, who were killed by frightened communities before they could.
w37 | =w17 | embers | | So the real horror of the Wendigo might not be a monster in the trees. It might be what fear and hunger do to a group of people, trapped together, in the longest night of the year.

# ---- WHAT ABOUT THE ANTLERS? ----
w38 | a gaunt skeletal figure with a deer skull and large antlers, crossed out with a bold red X | pulse | What about the antlers? | Quick myth check. If you've seen a Wendigo in a game or a horror film, it probably had a deer skull and huge antlers.
w39 | =w03 | zoomin night | | Look in the old accounts, and the antlers aren't there. Johnston's Weendigo is a starving giant with torn lips, not a deer. The antlers are a modern addition, from horror movies and video games. Spooky, sure. But it isn't the legend.

# ---- THE VERDICT ----
w40 | =w01 | zoomin snow night | | So how do you survive the Wendigo? Strangely, this is the one legend where none of the wards are about running, or hiding, or salt, or iron.
w41 | =w17 | embers zoomout | | Share your food. Never cross the line. Watch yourself. Keep your heart warm. And listen to the people who know these woods.
w42 | =w09 | breathe embers | | The Wendigo is a warning about what happens when anyone, a person, a family, a whole society, takes more than it needs and still wants more. And that, honestly, is a monster that never went away.
w43 | =w06 | dust zoomin | | Next week, we leave the frozen forest for a darker kind of wood. A hut that stands on chicken legs. A fence made of bones. And an old woman who might help you, or might put you in the oven.
w44 | an old witch flying in a wooden mortar steering with a pestle over a dark forest, full figure | endcard bats night | Baba Yaga | It's Baba Yaga. And she has very particular rules about the questions you're allowed to ask. Subscribe, so the Ward Book is open when she comes. And until then, share your dinner.
